.\" veetee(1) - the manual page for veetee.
.\" Every option in `veetee --help` is described here; a test in
.\" crates/veetee/src/cli.rs checks it.
.TH VEETEE 1 "September 2026" "veetee" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
veetee \- DEC VT terminal emulator for OpenVMS and other DEC hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B veetee
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR MODEL ]
.RB [ \-\-record
.IR FILE ]
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.RI [ CONNECTION ]
.br
.B veetee \-\-list\-profiles
.br
.B veetee
.BR \-v | \-\-version
.br
.B veetee
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B veetee
emulates the DEC video terminals from the VT52 to the VT525, built from DEC's own programmer
references: the VT420 by default, with its LK401 keyboard, Set-Up screens, status line, fonts
drawn on DEC's character cells, and printer port.
Its defaults are DEC's factory Set-Up and OpenVMS conventions rather than xterm's.
.PP
It connects over Telnet, SSH (through the system's OpenSSH client), a serial line, LAT (DEC's own
protocol, on Linux), or runs a local shell or command.
Files are transferred with Kermit, from the window menu.
.PP
Set-Up (F3) is the terminal's own configuration, saved per model; the window menu holds what a
terminal on a desk would not have, such as saved connections, logs and where print jobs go.
.SH CONNECTIONS
With no connection named,
.B veetee
opens the saved connection marked as the default, or else your login shell.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
A saved connection, from the Connections window.
Options given with it override what it saved.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-profiles
List the saved connections, marking the default, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-shell
Your login shell, even where a default connection is set.
.TP
.BI \-\-telnet " HOST\fR[\fP:PORT\fR]"
Telnet, veetee's own client, with BREAK, NAWS and RFC 2217 COM Port Control.
Also written
.BR telnet:// \fIHOST\fR[ :\fIPORT\fR].
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " \fR[\fPUSER@\fR]\fPHOST"
SSH through the system's OpenSSH client, so
.I ~/.ssh/config
and agents apply.
Also written
.BR ssh:// [\fIUSER\fB@\fR]\fIHOST\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " DEVICE"
A serial line:
.I /dev/ttyUSB0
on Linux,
.I COM3
on Windows.
Its speed, data format and flow control come from Communications Set-Up (see
.BR "LINE OPTIONS" ).
The port is opened for exclusive use; on Linux, join the
.I dialout
group to open it without root.
.TP
.BI \-\-lat " NODE"
LAT to a node on the local Ethernet segment (Linux only), as the node announces itself.
.TP
.BI \-\-command " COMMAND"
Run
.I COMMAND
through the system shell
.RB ( "sh \-c" ,
or
.B cmd /C
on Windows).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-model " MODEL"
The terminal:
.BR vt100 ,
.BR vt102 ,
.BR vt220 ,
.BR vt320 ,
.B vt420
(the default),
.BR vt510 ,
.B vt520
or
.BR vt525 .
It decides the identity reported to the host, the Set-Up screens or menus, and what the terminal
can do.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
The TCP port for
.B \-\-telnet
or
.BR \-\-ssh .
.TP
.BI \-\-interface " NAME"
The Ethernet interface
.B \-\-lat
speaks on; needed only where more than one is up.
.TP
.BI \-\-service " NAME"
The LAT service to ask for; by default, the node's own name.
.TP
.B \-\-telnet\-binary
Negotiate 8-bit Telnet (BINARY).
OpenVMS answers it by setting the terminal PASSALL, where DELETE stops erasing, so it is off
unless asked for.
.TP
.BI \-\-record " FILE"
Record the session to
.I FILE
(a
.I .vtrec
recording) for replay and tests; Ctrl+Shift+M marks a checkpoint.
What the host sends is recorded, but not what is typed.
.TP
.B \-\-record\-keys
Record typed keys as well, passwords included.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Add the session's text to
.IR FILE ;
.B ~
and
.BR %Y ,
.BR %m ,
.BR %d ,
.BR %H ,
.B %M
and
.B %S
are expanded.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-timestamps
Start each logged line with the date and time.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-raw
Log the host's bytes as received, rather than the text.
.TP
.BI \-\-sessions " N"
Open 1 or 2 sessions; 2 splits the window, and F4 switches between them.
.TP
.BI \-\-phosphor " COLOUR"
.B white
(P4, the default),
.B green
(P1) or
.B amber
(P3).
.TP
.BI \-\-keymap " FILE"
The PC-to-DEC keymap to use, instead of the one saved from the Keyboard Map window or the
built-in LK401 map.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print veetee's version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SH LINE OPTIONS
Given with
.BR \-\-serial ,
these set the port; given with
.BR \-\-telnet ,
they ask a terminal server for those settings with RFC 2217 COM Port Control.
What they leave out comes from the saved Communications Set-Up, else DEC's factory settings,
shown in brackets; a saved connection keeps its own.
Set-Up then shows what the line is doing, and leaving it with new settings changes the line while
connected, as the host can on the VT500 models with DECSCS, DECSPP and DECSFC.
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-baud " RATE"
Bits per second (9600).
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-databits " N"
5, 6, 7 or 8 (8).
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-parity " P"
.B n
none,
.B e
even,
.B o
odd,
.B m
mark,
.B s
space
.RB ( n ).
.TP
.BI \-s ", " \-\-stopbits " N"
1 or 2 (1).
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-flow " F"
.B x
XON/XOFF,
.B h
RTS/CTS,
.B n
none
.RB ( x ).
.SH KEYS
The PC keyboard stands in for the LK401; the Keyboard Map window changes the mapping.
.TP
.B F1
Hold Screen: stops reading from the host, which flow-controls it.
.TP
.B F2
Print Screen: the page on a VT420, the scrolling region on a VT500 model, as Printer Set-Up's
Print Extent says.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F2
Auto print on or off.
.TP
.B F3
Set-Up.
.TP
.B F4
Session: moves the keyboard to the other session.
.TP
.B F5
Break.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F5
Answerback.
.TP
.B F6\-F12, Shift+F1\-F10
F6 to F20; Shift+F5 is Help and Shift+F6 is Do.
.TP
.B Num Lock, keypad /
PF1 (Gold) and PF2 (Help) in EDT and EVE.
.SH FILES
Settings are kept in the user's configuration directory,
.IR ~/.config/veetee :
.TP
.I setup\-MODEL\-sessionN.conf
Saved Set-Up for each model and session: the terminal's power-up settings, written by Save in
the Set-Up Directory.
.TP
.I profiles.toml
Saved connections, and which is the default.
.TP
.I keymap.toml
The keymap saved from the Keyboard Map window.
.TP
.I appearance.conf
The screen effects, visible bell and log timestamps chosen in the window menu.
.TP
.I printer.conf
Where print jobs go: a folder of PDFs, or a printer.
.TP
.I print\-settings.ini
The printer chosen with Print to Printer.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VEETEE_LAT_TRACE
A file to write a trace of LAT messages to;
.B VEETEE_LAT_TRACE_DATA
adds what each carried, typed characters included.
.TP
.B VEETEE_LAT_HELPER
The LAT helper to use, where it is not beside
.B veetee
or in its
.I libexec
directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B veetee \-\-telnet vms1
Telnet to vms1 as a VT420.
.TP
.B veetee \-\-ssh system@vms1 \-\-model vt520
SSH as a VT520.
.TP
.B veetee \-\-serial /dev/ttyUSB0 \-b 19200
A serial line at 19200 baud, the rest from Set-Up.
.TP
.B veetee \-\-telnet decserver:2001 \-\-baud 19200 \-\-parity e \-\-flow h
A terminal server's line by RFC 2217.
.TP
.B veetee \-\-lat MYNODE
LAT to the node MYNODE.
.TP
.B veetee \-\-profile vms1 \-\-log '~/logs/vms1\-%Y%m%d.log' \-\-log\-timestamps
A saved connection, logged with timestamps.
.SH NOTES
On OpenVMS,
.B SET TERMINAL/INQUIRE
lets the host identify the terminal over Telnet, LAT, DECnet and serial lines; over SSH set the
device type instead, and add
.B /HOSTSYNC
so a long paste waits for the host.
The wiki's OpenVMS page has a LOGIN.COM for all of them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vt-headless (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR kermit (1),
.BR lp (1)
.PP
